 ("japanese","yamata_no_orochi","Yamata no Orochi","the eight-headed serpent of Izumo that ate the daughters, the eight vats of sake, the sword found in its tail that became one of the three regalia, the Hii river's floods as its reading, the Orochi in kagura today"),
 ("japanese","tengu","The Tengu","the bird-men of the mountains with the long noses and the fans, the corrupters of monks in the Konjaku, the teachers of martial arts (Yoshitsune at Kurama), the Chinese tiangou 'heavenly dog' name and the transformation, the tengu of the yamabushi, the Tengu-zōshi scrolls"),
 # ---------------- AMAZONIAN ----------------
 ("amazonian","ayots_avireri","Ayots / Avíreri (Mábireri)","the gigantic evil divinity of the Yanesha (Ayots), Ashaninka (Avíreri) and Nomatsiguenga (Mábireri) who carries his child-son on his shoulders and transforms people into stones, bees, termites and animals at the child's nagging questions; the drinking party and Armadillo's underground house where he is locked forever, holding up the earth and making earthquakes; his cannibal taste (people as edible larvae); Santos-Granero's argument (Ethnohistory 2002) that the myth is St Christopher inverted, as the mythical referent of the child-sorcery killings; the Pareni version of the Matsigenka; Weiss 1975, Smith 1977, Santos-Granero 1991"),
 ("amazonian","poporrona_kiri","Poporrona' / Kíri (Quërí)","the evil child who never grows, the son of Ayots (grandson of Avíreri), who asks 'who is that one?' until his father transforms the person, who turned his baby cousin into rotten wood and bathed in the river his father used to flood and eat the people, who could not be killed by arrows or clubs until he asked for a balsa throne and a hardwood spike through his head, and became the peach palm (Bactris); the name Kíri from Kiristu (Cristo) in Santos-Granero's reading; the model of the child witch (amaseñeñ, máci) and the executions of accused children (1880s-1960s) documented in the article"),
 ("amazonian","yos","Yos (Our Grandfather Yos, Yompor Yos)","the supreme creator of the Yanesha, father of Ayots and the other divinities, the Yompor (our father) figures of the Yanesha pantheon, Smith's and Santos-Granero's accounts, the temple-priesthood (cornesha') and the Palmazu pilgrimage centre with its stone divinities, the mimesis of the Mass reported by Bailley-Maitre (1902)"),
 ("amazonian","yosoper_korioshpiri","Yosoper / Korioshpiri","the evil chthonic ruler of all demons, father of witchcraft, Yosoper of the Yanesha and Korioshpiri of the Ashaninka, the suggested modelling on Lucifer (Smith, Weiss), the demons (ants, termites, wasps) as his helpers who bury the sorcerers' objects, the shamans' diagnoses"),
 ("amazonian","pareni","Pareni (the Salt Lady)","the Matsigenka woman who became the salt mountain (Cerro de la Sal), the mother who fed her sons-in-law and turned them into animals, Renard-Casevitz's transcription (1994) and its intertwining with the Ayots/Avíreri narrative, the Cerro de la Sal as the trade centre of the Selva Central and the Franciscan mission there"),
 ("amazonian","tasorentsi","Tasorentsi","the Ashaninka creator-blower ('the one who blows'), the transformer of the first times in Weiss's Campa Cosmology, the Tasorentsi as a class of powerful spirits and as the singular creator, the relation to Pavá and to the missionaries' God"),
 ("amazonian","pava","Pavá (the Sun)","the Ashaninka Sun as the father (Pavá means father), the source of the messiah's message of 1888 ('Father Sun had sent him'), the sun's ascent in the myths, the Adventist Sun-Father identification, the Yanesha Yompor Ror the sun"),
 ("amazonian","kashiri","Kashiri (the Moon)","the Ashaninka moon who came down as a man, gave manioc, married a woman and ate people (the cannibal moon whose child, the sun, burned his mother at birth), Weiss's accounts, the moon that eats the dead, the Yanesha Yachor Arrorr"),
 ("amazonian","itomi_pava","Itomi Pavá (the Son of the Sun)","the messianic figure of the Ashaninka: the divine messenger of 1888 (the white man in indigenous dress), Juan Santos Atahualpa's 1742 rising as its ancestor (the Inca who was Son of the Sun), the Adventist Stahl movement of 1928, Werner Bulner's claim c. 1960 to be Itomi Pavá, Brown and Fernández's War of Shadows"),
 ("amazonian","amachegua","The Amachegua","the divine messengers of the Ashaninka who appear at moments of crisis (1888 on the Pampas del Sacramento, 1897 in Pangoa), the Amachegua as a class between the Sun and the people, the risings convoked by them, the Shining Path years and the self-defence groups as the latest turn"),
 ("amazonian","kamari","The Kamari","the demons of the Ashaninka, the dead's malignant essences and the evil spirits of trees, rocks, ants and wasps, the kamari that cause illness by contact and in dreams, the shipokantaseri herbalists and the seripiari shamans against them, the peyari (the dead) as kin"),
 ("amazonian","maninkari","The Maninkari","the good spirits of the Ashaninka, invisible, who help the seripiari shaman through tobacco and ayahuasca, the maninkari of the hills and rivers, the shaman's transformation into a jaguar (Weiss), the maninkari and the Christian angels in the missions"),
 ("amazonian","chullachaqui","The Chullachaqui","the forest guardian with one foot uneven (goat, deer or backwards) who takes the shape of a friend and leads people into the forest, the Shapingo, the pact for hunting luck, the Loreto and Ucayali tellings, the Chullachaqui in Peruvian cinema and comics"),
 ("amazonian","yacuruna","The Yacuruna","the water-people of the river bottom with the cities and the backwards clothes, the abductors of women, the source of the shamans' powers (the yacuruna as teachers under the water), the boa and the dolphin as their forms, the Iquitos mestizo lore"),
 ("amazonian","tunche","The Tunche","the whistling spirit of the forest at night, the soul in pain or the devil, the whistle that must not be answered, the Tunche of the Peruvian selva and the Chanchamayo colonists' fear"),
 ("amazonian","sachamama","Sachamama","the giant serpent-mother of the forest so old that trees grow on her back, the hypnotic breath, the Yacumama's sister, the shamans' teacher, the Asháninka and mestizo versions"),
 ("amazonian","yacumama","Yacumama","the mother of the waters, the enormous river serpent of the Amazon and the Marañón, the whirlpools and the floods, the boa as her form, the Cobra Grande of Brazil as cognate, the anaconda in the ayahuasca visions"),
 ("amazonian","ayaymama","The Ayaymama","the bird whose cry is the lament of the abandoned children ('ay ay mamá'), the origin tale of the orphans left in the forest and turned into birds, the potoo (Nyctibius) as the bird, the Loreto and San Martín tellings"),
 ("amazonian","runamula","The Runamula","the woman turned mule for sleeping with a priest, the fire-breathing mule that runs through the towns at night, the Loreto and Ucayali versions, the colonial priest as the sin"),
 ("amazonian","bufeo_colorado","The Bufeo colorado","the pink river dolphin who becomes a handsome white man in a hat (to hide the blowhole) and seduces women at the fiestas, the dolphin's children, the dolphin as shaman and enemy, the Amazon-wide boto encantado, the pusanga love-magic made from its parts"),
 ("amazonian","shapshico","The Shapshico (Shapingo)","the devil-like forest demon of the Peruvian selva, the goat foot and the hairy body, the Chullachaqui's darker relative, the Huánuco and Pasco tellings, the Spanish devil's absorption"),
 ("amazonian","pishtaco","The Pishtaco (Ñak'aq, Kharisiri)","the pale stranger who kills to render human fat for church bells, cannon grease, medicines and machines: the sixteenth-century Cristóbal de Molina's account of the Indians' fear of the Spaniards' fat-taking, the ñak'aq of the Quechua highlands and the kharisiri of the Aymara (the friar with the sleeping powder), the pishtaco of the selva, the 2009 Huánuco fat-murders case and its collapse, Ansión's Pishtacos: de verdugos a sacaojos (1989), Weismantel's Cholas and Pishtacos, the gringo as pishtaco today"),
 # ---------------- LAKOTA ----------------
 ("lakota","wakan_tanka","Wakan Tanka","the Great Mystery as the totality of the sixteen wakan beings (Walker's Sword and Finger accounts) rather than a single god, the Tobtob (the four times four), the missionary and Black Elk shapings toward a Christian God, the debate (DeMallie) on what the concept was before 1880, the 'Great Spirit' translation's history"),
