 ("lakota","wi","Wi","the Sun, the chief of the superior gods, the husband of Hanwi shamed by Ite, the Sun Dance as his ceremony (the piercing, the ban of 1883 and the return), the sun in the winter counts"),
 ("lakota","hanwi","Hanwi","the Moon, Wi's wife who hid her face after Ite took her place at the feast, the separation of night and day as the punishment, the moon's phases as her shame"),
 ("lakota","tate","Tate","the Wind, the husband of Ite, the father of the four winds (the Wani) and of Yumni the whirlwind, the establishment of the directions and the year, Dooling's The Sons of the Wind retelling of the Walker texts"),
 ("lakota","ptesan_win","Ptesan Win (White Buffalo Calf Woman)","the bringer of the sacred pipe and the seven rites, the two scouts and the one who was turned to bones, the white buffalo calf as she left, the pipe kept at Green Grass (Arvol Looking Horse), Black Elk's account in The Sacred Pipe, the white buffalo births as signs (Miracle, 1994)"),
 ("lakota","iktomi","Iktomi","the spider trickster, the first-born son of Inyan (Ksa, wisdom, made foolish), the teller of the stories that must be told in winter, Ella Deloria's Dakota Texts, the Iktomi tales and the lessons, the modern Iktomi of the Lakota writers"),
 ("lakota","iya","Iya","the devouring giant, the second son of Inyan, the eater of people and the storm, the brother of Iktomi, the Iya of the Walker myths and of the folk fear (the whirlwind and the cyclone)"),
 ("lakota","unktehi","Unktehi","the water monsters of the rivers and lakes, the enemies of the Thunder Beings, the Unktehi of the Dakota accounts (Pond, Riggs), the buffalo-horned serpent, the fossils of the Badlands read as their bones, the Unktehi and the Ghost Dance"),
 ("lakota","wakinyan","The Wakinyan (Thunder Beings)","the winged thunder of the west, the enemies of Unktehi, the heyoka contraries who dream of them and must clown, the four Wakinyan of the Sword account, the Thunder Beings in the Sun Dance and the vision quests, Black Elk's great vision, the Harney Peak (Black Elk Peak) and the Black Hills"),
 # ---------------- ABRAHAMIC ----------------
